From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A locked navigation bar answers only a swipe up from the bottom edge

`immersive.navigation=*` keeps the navigation bar hidden while a reader uses an
application, but nothing about it is a lock. Three different things bring the bar
back on their own:

  - the swipe up from the bottom edge, whose AOSP thresholds are the status bar
    height for start and distance alike (`SystemGesturesPointerEventListener`,
    24 dp here) -- a scroll gesture that happens to start near the bottom edge is
    enough, and the bar then sits on top of the reader's own bottom 90 px;
  - the power-button panic gesture: turning the screen off and on again within
    `config_immersive_mode_confirmation_panic` (5 s) reveals both bars;
  - every other transient-bar request (a hovering mouse, the pending-panic path).

This patch adds the state the reader can ask for: a Settings.Global entry
`readom_nav_locked` of our own -- a literal name, not a new constant in
`Settings.java`, because a new constant would change framework.jar and the ART
boot image with it, and every application in /data would be recompiled at the next
upgrade. When it is 1:

  - the bottom edge swipe is tightened to 12 dp from the edge, 120 dp of travel and
    600 ms, and is the only thing that may reveal the bar; the panic gesture, the
    pending-panic path and every other transient request are refused;
  - a reveal is taken away again after 2 s, and at once if the finger leaves within
    250 ms of the reveal (a flick, not a request to use the bar);
  - SystemUI's hide button (patch 0077) asks for the state and for the bar to go
    away now through the broadcast `org.readom.policy.SET_NAV_LOCK`; that same
    button's long press asks for it back, which also drops `policy_control` so the
    bar stays up until the next boot.

`BarController.hideTransientNow()` is the one new method on the policy side: a
transient bar cannot be hidden with `setBarShowingLw(false)` alone, because its own
state machine keeps it up until the request is denied.

Everything above is inert unless `readom_nav_locked` is 1. The boot service writes
it only on this model (`nav=hide` in readom.conf), so a build of this patch for PW3
behaves exactly as it does today.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; the verification list is in
`readom-port/reports/t09-nav-impl.md`.

